Abstract— Although in-vehicle interaction technologies offer assistances for drivers from multi-aspects, the distractions that may cause by these technologies would significantly challenge driving safety. This study examined the influences of three in-vehicle interactions on drivers’ driving performance and eye movement behavior. The three in-vehicle interactions include phone pick-up (PPU), Bluetooth and audio (BT+AUD) and Bluetooth and head-up display (BT+HUD). A control group (i.e., None, in which no human-machine interaction was included while driving) was designed to highlight the influences of the examined interactions. Fourteen licensed drivers participated in the simulation experiments. Their age ranged from 20 to 30 years old (Mean ± SD = 25.1 ± 3.8). They were required to complete the Location Based Service (LBS) tasks using the three interactions while driving. Both driving performance and eye movement data were collected for analysis. Results showed that, when using PPU or BT+HUD, the minimum throttle opening, the steering wheel angle (SWA) and the Shannon entropy (SE) of SWA were all greater than those values when using BT+AUD or None. As for the SE of throttle opening and all the examined eye movement features (frequency and duration of fixation/saccade), the values when using BT+AUD or BT+HUD were higher than the values when using PPU or None. The presented results in this study provide evidences for car manufactures to design better BT+AUD human-machine interaction systems as a priority strategy.
Index Terms— Driving performance, eye movement, in-vehicle interactions, human factors, driving behavior
I. INTRODUCTION
With the rapid development of in-vehicle interactive technologies and the accessibility to portable electrical devices, there is an increasing trend for drivers to engage in non-driving-related tasks (NDRTs) while driving [1-4]. NDRTs can lead to driver distraction or inattention by competing for a variety of resources (e.g. visual, manual or cognitive) with the major driving task. According to [5], distraction associated with NDRTs has become one major cause of road traffic accidents. Therefore, minimizing the distracting effect of NDRTs is of great importance to today’s road safety. This can be achieved by improving the interface used when performing NDRTs [6].
