The most common interaction when performing NDRTs is the visual-manual interaction. Yet such interaction is not always safe considering that it usually requires drivers to take their eyes off the road to visually read messages, and to take their hands off the steering wheel to operate buttons. It has been well documented that, when engaging in visual-manual based NDRTs, drivers tend to have longer reaction times [7], reduced longitudinal and lateral control [8], and a higher scanning randomness [9]. These adverse effects on driving promotes the need for less distracting interactions, thus speech-based system have been introduced as a possible solution.
The speech-based interaction has become available with the development of voice-recognition technologies. It has an advantage over visual-manual interaction by minimizing the manual manipulation of input devices and glances away from the road. Speech-based interaction has been consistently reported as less disruptive to driving than visual-manual based interaction in the driving literature [10][11]. For instance, He et al. found that drivers showed better vehicle controlling performance when entering text messages through speaking than through typing [11]. Using meta-analysis, Simmons et al. reviewed studies that had compared the effects of two interaction and concluded that drivers had better performance with the speech-based system on reaction times, lane positioning and headway [12].
It should be noted that [12] have assumed that the speech-based systems from different studies all had similar effect size although these systems are not exactly the same. However, recent evidence suggested that different types of speech-based systems varied in their influences on driving performance. Reimer et al. carried out a simulated driving experiment to investigate whether accessing speech-based interaction through portable devices and embedded systems had similar distracting effects [13]. In either way, drivers needed to press a button to activate the system first and then vocally entered a dialing command. Their results suggested that embedded vehicle systems advantaged over portable device across a number of metrics such as lower workload, fewer task errors and shorter task completion time. However, their effects on driving performance had not been directly investigated.
While speech-based interface seems to be a promising solution to the distraction caused by NDRTs engagement, it is not totally distraction-free [14-16]. This is especially true when using speech feedback as the output of human-machine interaction. First, understanding the speech output can pose cognitive demand that could undermine driving performance. For instance, listening to emails while driving increased driver reaction time by 30% [14]. Another disadvantage associated with the speech-based output is that it usually takes longer
