To sum up, although previous literatures suggested speech-based interactions are promising ways to render NDRTs, their effects on driving performance and visual search behavior have not been understood well. This study aims to partially fill up this gap by investigating how three promising interaction modes would influence vehicle manipulation as well as attention distribution.
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section II introduces the details about the simulation experiment and the analyzed data. Section III presents the results found in this study. Section IV discusses the advantages and disadvantages of each interaction type by explaining why the interaction type leads to the better or worse results. Section V concludes this paper and proposes suggestions on the possible applications and future efforts.
II. METHOD
A. Apparatus
The simulation experiments presented in this study were conducted on a fixed-base driving simulator in Beihang University, Beijing, China. See Figure 1 for the simulator. The simulated roadway design and traffic scenarios were generated by UC-Win/Road Ver.8 Standard (FORUM8, Tokyo, Japan) with a refreshing rate of 20 Hz. The participants were required to complete a Location Based Service (LBS) driving task using different interactions while driving. An Apple iPhone 5s (screen size: 4 inches; resolution: 1136 x 640) operated by iOS 7 was used to conduct the LBS tasks. Smart Eye Pro Revision 5.9 was used to collect drivers’ eye movement data.
Figure 1. The used driving simulator in this study
B. Participants
Fourteen licensed drivers including twelve males and two females participated in this study. Their age ranged from 20 to 30 (Mean ± SD = 25.1 ± 3.8), which ensured that the participants were familiar with the usage of smart phones. A consent form approved by the local ethics committee was
signed prior to the experiments. Each participant was paid 100 RMB as a compensation for their efforts involved in the experiment.
C. Interactions and Tasks
Three human-machine interaction types were examined in this study, i.e., phone pick-up (PPU), Bluetooth and audio (BT+AUD), and Bluetooth and head-up display (BT+HUD). The Bluetooth module was flexibly mounted on the steering wheel with a preferred position by the driver. As long as an iPhone with the Bluetooth module was connected, Siri could be simply activated by pressing the button mounted on the steering wheel (See Figure 2).
Figure 2. The Bluetooth module mounted on the steering wheel
Using PPU, BT+AUD or BT+HUD, the LBS task required drives to search a designated place (e.g., gas station, restaurant, hotel, etc.), select a destination with a designated criterion (e.g., the nearest), and present the navigation route. For example, a given LBS task would be ‘please find the nearest gas station’. The details on how to use the three interactions to complete the LBS task are listed below:
(1) Phone pick-up (PPU)
Drivers use PPU to complete an LBS task following the steps: 1) Drivers press the button to activate Siri; 2) Drivers tell Siri what they want to search; 3) Drivers wait for the search results to present on the iPhone screen; 4) Drivers select the target destination on the screen to wait till the navigation route is ready.
(2) Bluetooth and audio (BT+AUD)
Drivers use BT+AUD to complete an LBS task following the steps: 1) Drivers press the button to activate Siri; 2) Drivers tell Siri what they want to search; 3) Siri reads the search results one by one, and drivers check each option by telling Siri ‘Next’ if the current option is NOT the desired destination or ‘Go there’ if it is the desired one; 4) Siri prepares and generates the navigation route.
(3) Bluetooth and head-up display (BT+HUD)
Drivers use BT+HUD to complete an LBS task following the steps: 1) and 2) are the same with the first two steps when using BT+AUD; 3) Siri presents the search results list on the head-up display area of the windshield; 4) Drivers tell Siri the rank of the target destination on the list (for example, drivers would say ‘Go to the Second’), and Siri prepares and generates the navigation route. See Figure 3 for an overview of how the BT+HUD interacts with drivers while driving.
