III. RESULTS
Both driving performance and eye movement measures were compared between the four examined interaction situations (i.e., None, PPU, BT+AUD, BT+HUD) using the repeated measures ANOVA. LSD tests were adopted to examine the statistical significance between each two of the examined groups in the post hoc tests. The statistical significance level was selected to be 0.05.
A. Driving Performance
The Min throttle opening value showed statistical significance among the examined interaction groups. See Figure 4. The Min throttle opening when using BT+AUD was almost the same with the None interaction group. The value when using PPU was 80% greater than that when using BT+AUD (p=0.008). The value when using BT+HUD was 1.6 times of than when using BT+AUD (p=0.035). No statistical significances were found in the other throttle features (i.e., Mean, Med and Max) among the interaction groups. The pairwise comparison results showed that the Max throttle opening in the None interaction group was 20% greater than that when using PPU (p=0.033). See Table II for more detailed information about the pairwise comparison tests. No statistical significances were found between any two interaction groups in the pairwise comparison tests for either Med or Mean.
