As illustrated in Figure 6(a), the SE of SWA in the None interaction group was the lowest, which indicates a stable SWA operation pattern to keep the subject vehicle driving in the lane. The SE of SWA in the BT+AUD interaction group was better than this feature in PPU or BT+HUD. Statistical significance was found in the SE of SWA among the examined interaction groups (p=0.006). This better lateral performance was consistent with the results found in Figure 5. Interestingly, the SE of throttle opening in the BT+AUD group ranked the highest among the examined interactions, which indicates that drivers operate the throttle more frequently in the BT+AUD group than in the other groups. Statistical significance was found among the examined interactions (p<0.001). See Figure 5(b). The higher SE of throttle operation but lower SE of SWA when using BT+AUD probably indicate that although drivers worked in a high-workload situation (i.e., the LBS task in this study), the BT+AUD interaction could prevent drivers from distracting too much by the NDRTs to keep safe longitudinal and lateral performances.
