IV. DISCUSSION
When using PPU, drivers need to move their attention allocation from the surrounding road traffic to the smart phone, which will significantly distract the drivers. The moving distance of drivers’ attention allocation is the longest when using PPU. According to the Fitts’ Law originally proposed by Paul Maurice Fitts in 1954 [21], when the size of the target area is determined, the time humans needed to transit from the original allocation to the target area is proportional to the transition distance. Besides, drivers also need to move and/or turn their head from the forward road to focus on the smart phone for the LBS task. The head movement would increase the difficulty and workload of LBS task when using PPU compared with the other interactions, thereby the high workload would degrade drivers’ situation awareness. Such degraded situation awareness level would easily lead to worse longitudinal and lateral driving performances [22], less frequent fixations and saccades, and shorter durations of the fixations and saccades [18].
When using BT+HUD, neither do drivers need to turn their heads with large angles nor do they need to move their attention allocations with long distances. However, the displayed information on the screen when doing the LBS task will partially occupy drivers’ physiological capabilities that are assigned to deal with visual information. The rest capability to deal with road traffic situations will be restricted. Thus, the longitudinal and lateral driving performances will be degraded when using BT+HUD. Whereas the higher frequencies and longer durations of eye movement features indicate that drivers were driving with higher situation awareness levels when doing the LBS tasks using BT+HUD. In conclusion, when doing NDRTs like LBS, BT+HUD can help keep a reasonable situation awareness level but will degrade drivers’ driving performance. Similar results were found in [16].
When using BT+AUD, drivers are released from getting NDRT information from the visual channel. Thus, they could concentrate on keeping driving safely to get the best driving performance among the examined interactions. As for the situation awareness levels in the examined interaction groups, both BT+HUD and BT+AUD can help drivers keep aware of the potential dangers. That’s because NDRTs would usually make drivers stressed and be more conscious of the surrounding traffic situations, so that they would check with higher frequencies and longer durations. However, the PPU solution overloads drivers so that they cannot keep aware of the traffic situations with appropriate visual search behavior.
