V. CONCLUSION
This study examined drivers’ driving performance and eye movement patterns when searching a required destination on a smart phone in four different interaction situations, i.e., None, PPU, BT+AUD and BT+HUD. Results showed that the Min throttle opening value when using PPU or BT+HUD was greater than that when using BT+AUD or None. Similar results were found for the SWA and the SE of SWA. Concerning the SE of throttle opening and all the examined eye movement features, the values when using BT+AUD or BT+HUD were higher than the values when using PPU or None. To conclude the comparison results concerning the driving performance and eye movement features when using the examined interactions, BT+AUD is the best and BT+HUD ranks the second. PPU is the worst interaction type. The results presented in this study provide evidences for car manufactures to design better BT+AUD human-machine interaction systems instead of BT+HUD systems. A well-designed BT+AUD interaction system could assist drivers without distracting them too much to keep an appropriate workload and situation awareness level while driving.
For future studies, the following topics are suggested to be further considered: (1) This study does not examine the performance of the interaction types on highways. As highway driving is another key element in drivers’ practical driving experience, the investigation on the effectiveness of the interactions on highways is a solid requirement [24]. (2) Field operation tests are needed to re-examine the findings concluded from simulation experiments [25], so that a more solid conclusion could be drawn to encourage safe and reliable applications in vehicles.
